// The committed set — 25 queries, `plans/06` phase 3. Fixture corpus only: no
// real prompt text, no real project, client or path appears in this file or in
// evals/fixture/. The private reference set lives in ~/.potsherd/evals/.
//
// Every query is written the way somebody remembers a conversation weeks
// later: the thing it was *about*, not the words they typed at the time. The
// first version of this set failed that test — "webhook rate limited by the
// gateway" against a prompt saying "the outbound webhook is getting rate
// limited by their gateway" measures substring overlap, not retrieval, and
// scored 10/10 with every answer at rank 1 over eleven candidates. The runner
// now prints the word overlap between each query and its answer so that
// failure cannot come back quietly; see `--overlap` in evals/run.ts.
//
// `class` records what each query is for:
//
//   concept   the answer shares almost no content words with the query. bm25
//             can genuinely fail here; this is where the vector half earns its
//             350 ms.
//   ranking   the words appear, and so do they in three or four distractor
//             sessions on adjacent topics. Getting it right is ordering.
//   recall    the ordinary case: a couple of distinctive words remembered
//             correctly, competing against a corpus that shares them.
//
// `needs` records *where in the index the answer physically is*, which is what
// `plans/06` phase 3 asks the set to cover: 5 answerable only from a sidechain,
// 5 answerable only from a ghost, 5 answerable only from a card.
//
//   sidechain  the words exist only in a subagent transcript; the parent
//              session never says what came back
//   ghost      the session was deleted. history.jsonl kept the prompts and
//              nothing else — there is no assistant text to match
//   card       the exchange text does not answer the question and the title
//              does not either. Only the card's summary does, which is the
//              "what was that session about" case
//   text       the ordinary exchange body
//
// `expected_sidechain` asserts the answer came back as the subagent
// transcript, not merely as some session whose id starts the same way — a
// claude sidechain id is `<parent>:agent-<hash>`, so a prefix test alone
// cannot tell the two apart.
